.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yume
is the YUME client. It opens an authenticated carrier to
.BR yumed (8)
and sends local application streams through that carrier. The mandatory 2.0
carrier remains HTTP/2 for the full connection and carries masked WebSocket
binary messages after a Chrome-shaped page/asset prime and RFC 8441 extended
CONNECT. Its mandatory inner channel combines ephemeral ML-KEM-1024, X25519,
and a random file PSK with salted HKDF, then uses one-use AES-256-GCM message
keys and independent directional epochs. AUTH v2 verifies the composite
Ed25519 and ML-DSA-87 client identity and binds authentication and the key
schedule to the live TLS exporter. The client authenticates the server through
TLS trust, optional leaf pinning, and optional operator proof.
.PP
The product version is 0.3.0-dev1; these commands speak transport-v2 wire
0.2.0-dev6. The experimental YTP/1 replacement and its schema-1 tools are
separate. A schema-1 configuration does not drive this client.
.PP
The client can expose a local SOCKS5 proxy, run one command through the
tunnel, create local or reverse forwards, attach to relay clients, or
use the directory and chat/file control features when the server permits
them.
.SH ROUTING MODEL
The common direct route is:
.PP
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  browser / curl                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  TLS / H2 / YUME frames        |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ==YUME==>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  direct egress                 |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees server IP                |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
The client network normally sees an HTTPS-looking connection to the YUME
server. The target normally sees the YUME server's egress IP. The server
process receives target metadata because it must open the outbound target
socket unless the application itself routes through another layer such as
Tor.
.SS Client through Tor to a hidden\-service yumed
With
.B \-\-tor
or
.B \-\-proxy socks5://host:port
the client routes the YUME carrier through a SOCKS5 proxy (typically the
local Tor daemon). The destination hostname is sent as ATYP_DOMAIN so
.B .onion
targets resolve on the proxy side; no DNS leaks. On the server,
.B yumed
binds 127.0.0.1 and Tor publishes a HiddenService that forwards onion
traffic to it.
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  browser / curl                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | SOCKS, --run, or forward
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  --tor / --proxy socks5://...  |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | SOCKS5 to <onion>:443
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  LOCAL TOR                     |
|  127.0.0.1:9050                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | encrypted cells
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR CIRCUIT                   |
|  hidden-service rendezvous     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | rendezvous on server
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  SERVER TOR                    |
|  hidden service publisher      |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | TLS 1.3 + YUME frames
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  binds 127.0.0.1 only          |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | outbound socket
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees yumed egress IP          |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
.SS Server-side Tor egress
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  normal app                    |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  YUME carrier                  |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ==YUME==>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  receives target request       |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  SERVER TOR                    |
|  SOCKS / transparent route     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ...>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR ENTRY                     |
|  obfs4 bridge or guard         |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR MIDDLE                    |
|  relay nodes                   |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR EXIT                      |
|  target sees this IP           |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  website / API                 |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
This makes the target see a Tor exit instead of the YUME server, but the
.B yumed
process still receives the requested target unless the application itself
is only asking the server to connect to a Tor entry.
.SS Tor over YUME
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR BROWSER                   |
|  or app configured for Tor     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  LOCAL TOR                     |
|  builds circuit                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  relays Tor TCP                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ==YUME==>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  opens Tor entry only          |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR ENTRY                     |
|  guard or obfs4 bridge         |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR NETWORK                   |
|  middle relays + exit          |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees Tor exit                 |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
For target anonymity from the YUME server, prefer this route. The YUME
server usually sees only a Tor guard or bridge, not the final website.
.SS YUME over Tor
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  normal app                    |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  carrier source                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  CLIENT TOR                    |
|  carries YUME carrier          |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ...>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR EXIT                      |
|  client-side edge              |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  sees Tor exit                 |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees server IP                |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
This hides the client's real IP from the YUME server, but it is slower
and the Tor exit can see that a connection is being made to the YUME
server.
.SS Tor -> YUME -> Tor
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  normal app                    |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  carrier source                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | YUME carrier through client Tor
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  CLIENT TOR                    |
|  obfs4 optional                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR EXIT A                    |
|  client-side edge              |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | YUME carrier reaches server
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  sees Tor exit A               |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | target stream through server Tor
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  SERVER TOR                    |
|  obfs4 optional                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR EXIT B                    |
|  target-side edge              |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees exit B                   |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
This can reduce linkability for passive observers because neither edge
sees both the real client and the final target. It is also much slower
and does not hide the requested target from
.B yumed
unless the application itself uses Tor over YUME.
.SS Common routes summary
.nf
Fast path:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> target

Server-side Tor egress:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> server Tor ...> target

Tor over YUME:
  Tor Browser -> local Tor -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> Tor entry ...> target

YUME over Tor:
  app -> yume -> client Tor ...> yumed -> target

Tor -> YUME -> Tor:
  app -> yume -> client Tor ...> yumed -> server Tor ...> target

VPN/proxy after YUME:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> VPN/proxy -> target
.fi
.SH TRANSPORT LAYERS
.SS Carrier frame shape
.nf
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  OUTER TLS 1.3 CARRIER                                               |
|                                                                      |
|  YUME frame header                                                   |
|    type, flags, stream id                                            |
|                                                                      |
|  INNER PAYLOAD                                                       |
|    encrypted with BaseFWX / AES-256-GCM                              |
|    authenticated with frame context                                  |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
Inner encryption protects YUME frame payloads between the client and
server. It does not replace application HTTPS and does not hide target
metadata from a server that is asked to open the target directly.
.SS HTTP/2 obfs sequence
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  TLS HANDSHAKE                 |
|  real TLS 1.3                  |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  HTTP/2 PREFACE                |
|  PRI * HTTP/2.0                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  PROJECT H2 SETTINGS           |
|  SETTINGS + WINDOW_UPDATE      |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  OBFUSCATED REQUEST            |
|  POST /token/nonce HEADERS     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME FRAMES                   |
|  carrier data begins           |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
The obfs path uses a standards-conformant HTTP/2 opening before the YUME
carrier begins. Its settings and headers are project templates, not
byte-identical output from a named browser, and the later carrier is not a
full-session HTTP/2 tunnel.
.SS Stream multiplexing
.nf
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  ONE AUTHENTICATED CARRIER                                           |
|                                                                      |
|  stream 1  OPEN ifconfig.me:80  -> DATA -> DATA <- CLOSE             |
|  stream 2  OPEN 1.1.1.1:443   -> app TLS bytes <-> target bytes      |
|  stream 3  OPEN api.site:443  -> another independent connection      |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
.fi
.SH SHARE FILES
.TP
.BI "export " file.yss
Encrypt the merged client configuration, composite identity, and selected trust
material into a password-protected YUME secure-store file. The destination is
created exclusively with mode 0600; existing paths, symlinks, and non-regular
entries are refused. Output is capped at 16 MiB and a partial publication is
removed on failure.
.TP
.BI "import " file.yss
Read and decrypt a YUME secure-store file, then publish its extracted private
files and ready-to-use configuration beneath the imported-profile directory.
Input is limited to 16 MiB before parsing or password-KDF work. Plaintext,
password, and bundled secret buffers are wiped on success and failure.
.TP
.B --password-stdin
Read the share password from standard input for non-interactive export or
import. Avoid command-line password arguments, which can be visible in process
listings.
.SH MODES
.TP
.BI "--socks " [addr:]port
Start a local SOCKS5 proxy. The optional
.I addr
must be an IP literal, for example
.BR 127.0.0.1:1080 ,
.BR 0.0.0.0:1080 ,
or
.BR [::1]:1080 .
Without an address YUME keeps the historical wildcard bind. Use
.B socks5h://
in clients that support it so DNS is resolved through YUME.
.TP
.BI "-L " [bind:]lport:host:port
Open an SSH-style local forward. Local connections to
.I lport
are relayed through YUME to
.IR host : port .
The optional
.I bind
must be an IP literal.
.TP
.BI "-R " [bind:]rport:host:port
Open a reverse forward. The server listens on
.I rport
and relays accepted connections back to the client's
.IR host : port .
The optional
.I bind
must be an IP literal on the server side.
.TP
.BI "--lport " port ", --rhost " host ", --rport " port
Long-form spelling of a local forward. Local connections to
.I lport
are relayed through YUME to
.IR host : port .
All three must be given together.
.B -L
is the compact spelling and also accepts a bind address. Without one,
the listener keeps the historical wildcard bind.
.TP
.BI "--run \fR, \fB-c " command
Run a local command with its TCP/UDP sockets routed through YUME.
.TP
.B --proxycmd
Act as a one-shot SOCKS5 client for another program's proxy hook. YUME connects
to a SOCKS listener on 127.0.0.1 at the
.B --socks
port, or 1080 when
.B --socks
is absent, issues a CONNECT for
.B --dest
and
.BR --dport ,
then relays standard input and output over that stream. This is the form
.B --run
generates as a
.BR ssh (1)
ProxyCommand. It is not supported on Windows.
.TP
.BI "--dest " host
Destination host for
.BR --proxycmd .
.TP
.BI "--dport " port
Destination port for
.BR --proxycmd .
.TP
.BI "--exec " command
Send an outbound EXEC request for
.I command
through the controlled-client mode selected by
.BR --control .
The request syntax is retained, but the current server answers it with an
explicit safety denial, so no command runs. The value is refused if it begins with
.BR - .
.TP
.B --allow-exec
Rejected. Inbound remote command execution is disabled until child processes
have bounded shutdown support, so passing this option fails argument parsing.
.TP
.B --directory
List visible relay endpoints from the authenticated server.
.TP
.BI "--chat " id-or-name
Start a chat with a relay endpoint when allowed by policy.
.TP
.BI "--send-file " id-or-name " " path
Send a file through the relay channel when allowed by policy.
.TP
.BI "--send-bytes " id-or-name " " path
Send raw bytes through the relay channel when allowed by policy.
.TP
.BI "--admin-attach " id-or-name
Open a trusted admin channel to a relay endpoint when both sides permit it.
.TP
.BI "--control " [id]
Attach to a controlled client.
.TP
.BI "--id " id
Controlled-client identifier for
.BR --control .
.TP
.B --list-controlled
List the controlled clients registered with the server.
.TP
.B --attach-local
Attach to a local running YUME instance.
.TP
.BI "--packet-tun " ifname
Attach packet-bulk mode to an operator-created Linux TUN interface. YUME does
not create routes, NAT rules, or the interface, and this exclusive data-plane
mode cannot be combined with SOCKS, forwards, relay, benchmark, or control
modes.
.TP
.B --monero-rpc
Start the built-in Monero RPC application codec and listen on
.BR 127.0.0.1:18089 .
A wallet reaches it with
.BR "--daemon-address 127.0.0.1:18089" .
Application codecs are protocol-aware local service shims, not raw TCP
forwards. The server must also permit the codec for this key.
.TP
.BI "--monero-rpc-listen " addr:port
Start the Monero RPC codec on an explicit listener. Equivalent to
.B --monero-rpc
together with
.BR --codec-listen .
.TP
.BI "--codec " name
Select an application codec by registry name. In the transport-v2 runtime the only built-in
codec is
.BR monero-rpc-v1 ,
which is also accepted as
.B monero-rpc
or
.BR monero .
.TP
.BI "--codec-listen " addr:port
Override the codec listener. The address must be a loopback IP literal and the
port must be 1-65535. The default is
.BR 127.0.0.1:18089 .
.TP
.B --quick-bench
Run the bounded local device benchmark smoke profile.
.TP
.B --full-bench
Run the extended local device benchmark profile. These local profiles are not
WAN, stealth, classifier, or release-qualification evidence.
.SH BENCHMARK OPTIONS
Two independent benchmark families exist. The endpoint benchmark runs against a
live server started with
.BR "yumed --bench" .
The local device profiles
.B --quick-bench
and
.B --full-bench
are listed under MODES. They need no server, identity, or configuration, and
they require a binary built with the selftest harness. Neither family is WAN,
stealth, classifier, or release-qualification evidence.
.TP
.B --bench
Run the authenticated endpoint upload/download benchmark against the server,
then exit. This also selects non-interactive output.
.TP
.B --bench-full
Run the longer endpoint profile. Unless overridden it uses 1024 MiB per
direction and 64 concurrent streams.
.TP
.BI "--bench-mib " n
Benchmark payload per direction in MiB. Non-positive values select the default
of 256; values above 16384 are clamped to 16384.
.TP
.BI "--bench-chunk-kib " n
Upload DATA chunk size in KiB. The default matches the SOCKS/relay read buffer,
currently 64 KiB. Non-positive values select that default; values above 256 are
clamped to 256.
Only the default chunk size reports a production stream shape.
.TP
.BI "--bench-streams " n
Concurrent benchmark streams. Default
.BR 1 ,
clamped to
.BR 1 - 240 .
.TP
.BI "--bench-direction " direction
Measured direction:
.BR both ,
.BR up ,
or
.BR down .
Default
.BR both .
.PP
The remaining options tune the local device benchmark and are forwarded to it.
They have no effect without
.B --quick-bench
or
.BR --full-bench .
.TP
.BI "--configs " list
Comma-separated config subset, or
.BR all .
The default runs the direct baseline and the mandatory yume-v2 stack.
.TP
.B --list-configs
Print the local benchmark config names and exit.
.TP
.BI "--duration-sec " n
Full-mode target duration hint in seconds. Range
.BR 30 - 600 ,
default
.BR 180 .
It scales the payload size.
.TP
.BI "--latency-iters " n
Echo round trips per config. Default 120. Full mode uses 360.
.TP
.BI "--bulk-mib " n
Bulk echo size per config in MiB. Quick mode defaults to 32. Full mode computes
a duration/config-count value and clamps it to 512-8192.
.TP
.BI "--streams " n
Concurrent bulk streams per config. Quick mode defaults to 1; full mode uses 64.
.TP
.BI "--client-threads " n
Client I/O threads. Use
.B 0
for automatic selection.
.TP
.BI "--server-threads " n
Server I/O threads. Quick mode defaults to 2. Full mode defaults to one quarter
of the detected logical CPUs, clamped to 4-16.
.TP
.BI "--cooldown-ms " n
Pause between configs for fairer sweeps. Quick mode defaults to 500; full mode
uses 1000.
.TP
.BI "--repeat " n
Run each config
.I n
times and report the median-throughput trial. Quick mode defaults to 1; full
mode uses 3.
.TP
.B --one-way
Measure one-way upload with sink and acknowledgement instead of echo.
Quick mode defaults to echo; full mode enables one-way measurement.
.TP
.BI "--json " path
Write the JSON result file to
.IR path .
.TP
.B --json-stdout
Print the JSON result to standard output after the table.
.TP
.B --dev
Show raw component tables, phase timings, and row details.
.TP
.B "--color, --no-color"
Force or disable ANSI colors in benchmark output.
.TP
.B --keep-workdir
Keep the temporary logs and generated keys the run creates.
.SH CONNECTION OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--server " host
Server hostname or IP address.
.TP
.BI "--port " port
Server port. If omitted, the client uses the configured/default server
port.
.TP
.BI "--cluster " host[:port]
Cluster entry-point short form for
.B --server
and
.BR --port .
Bracket IPv6 literals as
.BR [2001:db8::1]:443 .
.TP
.B --no-proxy
Ignore any outbound proxy carried by the configuration and dial the server
directly.
.TP
.BI "-i, --auth " path
Composite Ed25519 + ML-DSA-87 identity used to authenticate this client to the
server.
.TP
.BI "--secondary-auth " path
Composite identity for one data-only secondary SOCKS tunnel. Repeat exactly
.IR N-1
times with
.BI "--tunnels " N .
Every secondary must authenticate before the SOCKS listener is considered
ready; the primary admin factor is never replayed on these data-only tunnels.
.TP
.BI "--admin-auth " path
Distinct composite second identity for an admin session. It must be enrolled in
the server's admin store; presenting this key is the admin claim.
.TP
.BI "--config " path
Read client configuration from a JSON file.
.TP
.B --save-server
Write the effective merged client configuration back to the
.B --config
file before connecting, creating or updating that file.
.TP
.BI "--threads " n
I/O thread count. Use
.B 0
for automatic selection.
.TP
.BI "--rekey-window " n
Concurrent directional epoch offers this client accepts, and, capped by the
server's advertised depth, the number it keeps prepared for sending. Range
.BR 1 - 64 ,
default
.BR 8 .
Each prepared epoch adds one negotiated epoch byte budget per rekey round trip
on high-latency links. Under the default Extreme mode that budget is 256 KiB,
with independent limits of 512 frames and 500 ms sender-active time. The JSON
configuration key
.B security_mode
selects Extreme, Normal, Soft, or a complete bounded Ultimate policy; see
.BR docs/SECURITY_MODES.md .
.TP
.B --udp
Enable UDP forwarding.
.TP
.B --tcp
Force TCP-only behavior.
.TP
.B --allow-local-ip
Allow private, loopback, and link-local destinations where policy permits.
.TP
.B --run-ipv4
Prefer IPv4 behavior for
.BR --run .
.TP
.B --root
Keep root privileges instead of dropping them where supported.
.SH SECURITY OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--obfs-secret-file " path
Load the mandatory admission secret. The file must contain exactly 64
lowercase hexadecimal characters encoding 32 random bytes, with no newline
and no group/world permissions.
.TP
.BI "--inner-psk-file " path
Load the separate mandatory inner PSK in the same strict file format. It is
mixed by salted HKDF; Argon2 is not used. Ratchet modes never disable the
mandatory hybrid exchange, HKDF, or per-message AES-256-GCM keys.
.TP
.B "--require-operator-identity"
Require proof that the endpoint is authorized by the selected operator CA.
This authenticates the operator; it does not prove no logging or anonymity.
.TP
.BI "--operator-ca-cert " path
Operator CA certificate used to verify endpoint authorization.
.TP
.B --accept-monitoring
Answer the privacy-risk consent prompt in advance for a server that presents no
operator identity proof. An ordinary non-interactive connection must set this
or require an authorized operator, otherwise the connection is refused. The
endpoint benchmark is the deliberate prompt-free exception. On the CLI,
.B --require-operator-identity
takes precedence if both flags are supplied; the stricter facade JSON contract
rejects that redundant combination.
Consenting records an acknowledgement. It does not reduce what the operator can
observe.
.TP
.BI "--tls-ca " path
TLS CA certificate.
.TP
.BI "--tls-pin " sha256
Pin the server TLS certificate fingerprint.
.SH TLS STEALTH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--profile " name
Select the pinned Chrome 151 Linux profile. In the transport-v2 runtime,
.B chrome
is the only accepted value.
.TP
.BI "--transport-profile " id
Select the authenticated dev6 transport identity. The only accepted value is
.BR chrome151-node24-v1 .
.TP
.BI "--tls-backend " name
Select
.B openssl-chrome151
for the default in-process, patched-OpenSSL emitter,
.B chrome151
for the optional packaged Linux helper, or
.B openssl-diagnostic
for the explicit non-Chrome-parity diagnostic path. There is no silent
fallback.
.TP
.BI "--tls-helper " path
Use an explicit absolute helper path when
.B --tls-backend chrome151
is selected. Other backends reject this option. The launcher requires a regular
executable owned by root or the current user and rejects group/world-writable
files.
.TP
.BI "--tls-name " host
Set the TLS SNI, certificate-verification name, and HTTP Host identity
independently from the dial address. This is required when connecting by IP to
a certificate issued for a DNS name.
.TP
.B "--hide-in-the-crowd chrome"
Explicit spelling for the same pinned Chrome 151 HTTP User-Agent profile.
Other HTTP profiles are rejected by the transport-v2 runtime.
.TP
.B --tls-fingerprint-log
Log TLS fingerprint metrics for the explicit
.B openssl-diagnostic
backend. Other backends reject this diagnostic-only option.
.TP
.BI "--tls-fingerprint-log-path " path
Path for TLS fingerprint log output.
.TP
.B --tls-fingerprint-verify
Verify fingerprints against a test endpoint for the explicit
.B openssl-diagnostic
backend. Chrome backends use the emitted-byte wire gates instead.
.TP
.BI "--tls-fingerprint-test-endpoint " host
Host used for fingerprint verification tests.
.TP
.B --self-dpi
Warn when local carrier metadata stops matching the selected disguise profile.
The checks are warning-only observations of this client's own traffic and are
not a filtering-resistance claim.
.TP
.B --no-self-dpi
Disable the self-DPI warnings.
.TP
.BI "--outer-carrier-evidence " absolute-path
Write the exact-policy one-flow outer-carrier evidence bundle. This option is
admitted only for the documented pinned Chrome 151 H2 benchmark geometry and
does not by itself prove browser parity, classifier resistance, or release
qualification. See
.BR TRANSPORT_PROFILES.md .
.SH RELAY OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--name " slug
Display name for this relay endpoint.
.TP
.BI "--client-id " 32hex
Stable client endpoint ID.
.TP
.BI "--relay-mode " mode
Relay trust mode:
.B untrusted
or
.BR trusted .
.TP
.BI "--relay-trust-mode " mode
End-to-end relay-v2 peer identity policy.  The default
.B tofu
records the first identity only after a successful authenticated ordinary-channel
handshake.  The
.B pinned
mode refuses peers without an explicit pin.  Neither mode permits identity
replacement.
.TP
.BI "--relay-trust-dir " path
Owner-only relay-v2 peer identity store.  Entries are bound to the exact endpoint
ID and are opened without following links.
.TP
.BI "--relay-peer-pin " endpoint=64hex
Configure an explicit composite-identity SHA-256 pin.  This option is repeatable.
Admin channels require an explicit matching pin even when ordinary channels use
TOFU.
.TP
.B "--allow-inbound-admin, --deny-inbound-admin"
Allow or deny inbound admin attach requests.
.TP
.B "--allow-outbound-admin, --deny-outbound-admin"
Allow or deny outbound admin attach requests.
.TP
.B "--allow-chat, --deny-chat"
Allow or deny chat relay features.
.TP
.B "--allow-file, --deny-file"
Allow or deny file relay features.
.TP
.B "--allow-bytes, --deny-bytes"
Allow or deny byte relay features.
.TP
.BI "--relay-key-file " path
Protected JSON relay-key record produced by the relay key generator.
Bare base64 files are rejected.
.SH RUNTIME OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--tunnels " n
Open 1 to 16 parallel TLS tunnels. Counts above one require either a bulk-key
session policy or one distinct
.B --secondary-auth
identity for every secondary tunnel.
.TP
.BI "--instance " name
Runtime instance name.
.TP
.BI "--history-dir " path
Chat history directory.
.TP
.BI "--relay-receive-dir " path
Directory for received relay file/byte transfers. Files are created
exclusively with mode 0600 and never overwrite or follow an existing entry.
.TP
.B --no-history
Disable chat history.
.TP
.B --non-interactive
Disable live status redraw.
.TP
.B --live-status
Enable live status redraw.
.TP
.B --timing
Emit precise timing diagnostics in a Debug or RelWithDebInfo developer build.
Release and MinSizeRel binaries compile these hooks out and warn that the
option is unavailable.
.TP
.B --boring
Use minimal output.
.TP
.BI "--accept-server-control " [port]
Opt in to server-initiated bounded TCP stream opens. The client publishes a
reverse listener for
.B 127.0.0.1:22
and the server picks its own listen port automatically. An explicit
.I port
pins the server-side listener and must be
.BR 3000 - 30000 .
Command execution is not part of this contract.
.TP
.BI "--accept-server-control-min-port " port ", --accept-server-control-max-port " port
Bound the automatically chosen server-side listener range used when no explicit
.B --accept-server-control
port is pinned. The default range is
.BR 4100 - 8600 .
Positive values are clamped to 1-65535; a non-positive value keeps that side's
default. An inverted pair is swapped. Passing either option also enables
.BR --accept-server-control .
.TP
.B --service-streams-only
Embedder mode: keep only the ABI service streams. The session stays up without
a local-attach runtime, an interactive console, or any listener of its own.
.TP
.B --
Service-safe launch separator.
.SH INFORMATIONAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B "-h, --help"
Print the built-in option summary and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Print local component versions and crypto capabilities, then exit. Setting
.B YUME_UPDATE_CHECK=1
also enables the GitHub release check.
.TP
.B --credits
Print authorship credits and the bundled open-source component
acknowledgements.
.TP
.BI "--completion " shell
Print a shell completion script and exit. The only supported value is
.BR bash .
The subcommand form
.B "yume completion bash"
is equivalent.
.SH INTERACTIVE CONSOLE
When a session stays attached, the console accepts commands such as:
.PP
.nf
    help, status, directory, contacts, forget, invites, chat, send,
    send-file, send-bytes, accept, reject, history, history-delete,
    admin attach, admin status, admin sessions, admin stop, whoami, quit
.fi
.PP
.B contacts
lists every peer whose relay identity this client trusts, merged with the
current directory so each row shows presence as well as where its trust came
from
.RB ( tofu ,
.BR explicit ,
or
.BR configured ).
Trust is durable local state and the directory is a live overlay, so contacts
still list when the server is unreachable; presence then reads
.BR unknown .
.PP
.BI "forget " endpoint-id
removes a trust-on-first-use pin. It refuses a fingerprint named by the
configuration or one carrying a durable explicit marker: those record an
out-of-band authorization decision, which is reversed by changing the
configuration rather than from the console.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start a local SOCKS5 proxy:
.nf
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --socks 127.0.0.1:1080
.fi
.TP
Use curl with remote DNS through the proxy:
.nf
curl -x socks5h://127.0.0.1:1080 https://ifconfig.me
.fi
.TP
Run one command through YUME:
.nf
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --run "curl https://1.1.1.1"
.fi
.TP
Create a local forward:
.nf
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 -L 2222:127.0.0.1:22
.fi
.TP
Require operator identity proof:
.nf
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --socks 127.0.0.1:1080 \
     --require-operator-identity --operator-ca-cert /etc/yume/operator-ca.pem
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Inner encryption protects YUME frame payloads between the client and
server. It is not browser-to-target end-to-end encryption; HTTPS in the
application still provides that layer.
.PP
The YUME server needs enough target metadata to open outbound sockets.
If the server must not learn final targets, run Tor locally and route Tor
through YUME.
.PP
Use deployment-specific PQ key files.
.PP
Operator identity proof verifies a CA-authorized endpoint and the associated
mode asks the server to minimize identifying logs. It cannot prove that the
operator follows that policy, and does not remove hosting logs, payment
records, DNS records, browser fingerprints, logged-in accounts, or
time-correlation risks.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config/yume.json
Example local client configuration. Its keys form a closed set: an unknown
or retired key is a load error, never a silently ignored setting.
.TP
.I ~/.yume/id_ed25519
Common client identity key location.
.TP
.I /etc/yume/
Common system configuration directory.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/yume/
Installed documentation in packaged builds.
.SH INSTALLATION
From a build tree:
.PP
.nf
\&./ezbuild.sh
sudo cmake --install build
.fi
.PP
Manual man-page install:
.PP
.nf
sudo install -Dm644 docs/man/yume.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/yume.1
sudo mandb
.fi
.PP
Debian package builds produced by CPack install this page automatically.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yumed (8),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR tor (1)
.PP
Project documentation is installed under
.I /usr/share/doc/yume/
when built as a package.
